Anencephaly
Disabilities & Congenital Disorders
Absence of cranial vault, Absence of forebrain, Bulging eyes, Elevated maternal alpha-fetoprotein

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DCD-003
Reason for Visit: Palliative care symptom management for acute distress and feeding intolerance in a neonate.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Neonate, mother, and father
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported the neonate had sudden onset of frequent gagging, excessive oral secretions, and apparent discomfort over the last 3 hours. The assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period due to the intense psychological distress of the parents and the need to immediately palliate acute physical symptoms in a fragile, life-limited infant.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: He is gagging constantly and seems so unsettled; we don't know how to comfort him.
History of Present Illness: The parents report that the 3-day-old neonate, discharged home for palliative care, has developed acute feeding intolerance. Over the past 12 hours, attempted oral feeds have resulted in immediate regurgitation. In the last 3 hours, there has been a continuous accumulation of secretions leading to frequent gagging and grimacing. The parents note the infant has absent spontaneous purposeful movement and no established sleep-wake cycle, but the intact brainstem reflexes are causing the current distressing gagging episodes.
Functional Status: Totally dependent; receiving exclusive palliative care.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme parental grief and exhaustion; need for intensive psychological and practical support.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: Not indicated | HR: 145 bpm | Temp: 36.5 C | RR: 45 bpm | SpO2: 92% | Wt: 2.8 Kg
General: Neonate is swaddled; active grimacing and spontaneous irregular breathing noted.
Physical Exam: Examination of the head reveals complete absence of the cranial vault (acrania) and forebrain, with the area cerebrovasculosa covered by a thin membrane. The face shows bulging eyes resulting in a characteristic appearance. Cleft palate is present. Neurological exam reveals absent higher cortical functions but present brainstem reflexes including gagging and spontaneous respiration. Copious oral secretions are present in the oropharynx. Lungs are clear to auscultation.
Environmental Observations: Quiet, dimly lit room; appropriate thermal environment maintained by parents.
Medication Reconciliation: Prescribed comfort medications (oral morphine drops) available but not yet administered by parents out of fear.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Anencephaly (with acute secretion accumulation and gagging) - Status: Expected natural progression
Differentials:

Acute Respiratory Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of fever, absence of focal lung crackles, and the known structural inability to manage secretions.

Seizure Activity - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lack of rhythmic clonic movements or autonomic instability, with symptoms isolated to brainstem-mediated gagging.

Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to a soft, non-distended abdomen and normal meconium passage earlier in the day.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Parental psychological distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The presentation of acute gagging and grimacing in a neonate with a terminal congenital anomaly required urgent, compassionate clinical intervention. The objective findings of copious unmanaged secretions triggering brainstem reflexes confirmed that the infant was experiencing physical discomfort. This assessment could not be delayed until the next in-hours period because timely palliative symptom control is paramount, and the profound psychological distress of the parents required immediate, hands-on guidance to administer comfort medications and adjust care strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed and supervised the administration of prescribed oral morphine for comfort and glycopyrrolate to dry secretions. Demonstrated gentle oral suctioning techniques with a bulb syringe.
Positive Behavioral Change: When you use the bulb syringe gently on the inside of the cheek, the secretions clear and he rests easier; therefore, doing this proactively before feeds will give you a sense of control and provide him direct comfort. When you administer the comfort drops at the first sign of grimacing, you prevent escalation; making this a confident routine reinforces your ability to care for him.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent notification to the community pediatric palliative care team for a morning review.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided additional bulb syringes and soft oral swabs.
Follow-up: Community palliative care nurse visit scheduled for tomorrow morning.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Contact the 24/7 palliative care on-call number if the infant develops severe respiratory distress, uncontrollable pain behaviors, or if you feel completely overwhelmed and unable to cope.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical course is incompatible with sustained extrauterine life; symptoms are managed solely for comfort.
Clinical Course: Expected natural course is progressive decline in brainstem function leading to eventual respiratory cessation. Focus remains entirely on palliation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call the palliative care team if the secretions are not controlled by the medication within 2 hours, or if the grimacing and distress continue despite the morphine drops.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The glycopyrrolate will reduce the amount of saliva he produces, stopping the gagging. The morphine will ease any discomfort and help relax his breathing.
Actionable Steps: Administer the drying drops as charted. Use the bulb syringe gently for pooled saliva. Hold and comfort him as much as desired.

